# Normalize EOL for all files that Git considers text files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets are stored as ordinary Git objects, NOT in Git LFS. This is deliberate.
#
# There used to be two LFS rules here, written with brace expansion:
#
#   assets/organized/**/*.{png,jpg,...} filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
#
# gitattributes does not support braces - only fnmatch globs (*, ?, [], **) - so those lines
# matched nothing from the day they were written and every asset has always been a plain blob.
# The per-extension form is what actually works, if you ever need it:
#
#   assets/organized/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
#
# We removed them rather than repaired them. Packed history is ~59 MiB against ~66 MB for one
# checkout of assets/, so history costs barely more than a single copy: of 2120 tracked binaries
# only 167 have ever been modified, and each exactly once. Deduplicating revisions of large
# binaries is the whole point of LFS and there are almost none to deduplicate. Switching now
# would spend GitHub's 1 GB/month LFS bandwidth on every clone, rewrite every commit SHA to
# migrate the existing blobs, and introduce the pointer-file failure mode that cannot currently
# happen at all.
#
# Revisit if art iteration becomes routine - repeatedly re-exporting the same PNGs is exactly
# what LFS is for - or if the repo passes a few hundred MB. The largest file today is a 4 MB
# mp3 and GitHub's hard per-file limit is 100 MB, so there is plenty of runway.
